@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Home";
}

<h1>MVC sample</h1>

<p class="lead">
    This is the only page a signed-out visitor can reach, and only because its action carries
    <code>[AllowAnonymous]</code>.
</p>

<p>
    Every other page here is behind interactive login — not because it declares a policy, but because the
    package's gating middleware challenges any matched endpoint that is not explicitly anonymous. Follow one of
    the links above and you will be sent to Frontegg first.
</p>

<h2 class="h4 mt-4">Where the gates are</h2>

<table class="table table-striped">
    <thead>
    <tr><th>Page</th><th>Gate</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
    <tr><td><a asp-controller="Home" asp-action="Profile">Home/Profile</a></td><td>authenticated</td></tr>
    <tr><td><a asp-controller="Reports" asp-action="Index">Reports/Index</a></td><td><code>[PermissionAuthorize]</code> on the controller</td></tr>
    <tr><td><a asp-controller="Reports" asp-action="Create">Reports/Create</a></td><td>two stacked <code>[PermissionAuthorize]</code> — AND</td></tr>
    <tr><td><a asp-controller="Reports" asp-action="Export">Reports/Export</a></td><td><code>[PermissionIdAuthorize]</code> — numeric id</td></tr>
    <tr><td><a asp-controller="Reports" asp-action="Restricted">Reports/Restricted</a></td><td><code>ReversePermissions</code> — denies on presence</td></tr>
    <tr><td><a asp-controller="Reports" asp-action="Manage">Reports/Manage</a></td><td><code>[RoleAuthorize]</code>, one role synthesized by a claims transformer</td></tr>
    <tr><td><a asp-controller="Reports" asp-action="Help">Reports/Help</a></td><td><code>[SkipAuth]</code> — signed in, no permission check</td></tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<p class="text-muted">
    A page you are not allowed to see answers 403 with an RFC 7807 body rather than redirecting: by the time a
    filter runs you are authenticated, just not permitted.
</p>
